.TH IBV_RU_PINGPONG 1 "August 8, 2026" "libibverbs" "USER COMMANDS"

.SH NAME
ibv_ru_pingpong \- simple RU transport test

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ibv_ru_pingpong
[\-p port] [\-d device] [\-i ib port] [\-s size] [\-m size]
[\-r rx depth] [\-n iters] [\-e] [\-g gid index] [\-c] [\-N] [\-Q]
[\-I bits] [\-S sges] [\-\-pidonfep n] [\-\-ri n] \fBHOSTNAME\fR

.B ibv_ru_pingpong
[\-p port] [\-d device] [\-i ib port] [\-s size] [\-m size]
[\-r rx depth] [\-n iters] [\-e] [\-g gid index] [\-c] [\-N] [\-Q]
[\-I bits] [\-S sges] [\-\-pidonfep n] [\-\-ri n]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Run a simple ping-pong test using the reliable unconnected (RU) transport.
.PP
Invoked without a
.I HOSTNAME
the program acts as the server and waits for a connection; invoked with
one it acts as the client and connects to the server at that address.
Both sides allocate a job and a job key, create an RU queue pair, and
exchange messages with
.BR ibv_post_send (3)
or, with
.BR \-N ,
the work request builders described in
.BR ibv_wr_post (3).
.PP
The device must report
.I IBV_DEVICE_RU
in
.IR device_cap_flags_ex .
See
.BR ibv_uet (7)
for the underlying model.

.SH OPTIONS

.PP
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-port\fR=\fIPORT\fR
use TCP port \fIPORT\fR for initial synchronization (default 18515)
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-ib\-dev\fR=\fIDEVICE\fR
use IB device \fIDEVICE\fR (default first device found)
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-ib\-port\fR=\fIPORT\fR
use port \fIPORT\fR of the IB device (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-size\fR=\fISIZE\fR
exchange messages of size \fISIZE\fR (default 4096)
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-mtu\fR=\fISIZE\fR
path MTU \fISIZE\fR (default 1024)
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-rx\-depth\fR=\fIDEPTH\fR
post \fIDEPTH\fR receives at a time (default 500)
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-iters\fR=\fIITERS\fR
perform \fIITERS\fR message exchanges (default 1000)
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-events\fR
sleep while waiting for work completion events (default is to poll for
completions)
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-gid\-idx\fR=\fIGIDINDEX\fR
use local port GID index \fIGIDINDEX\fR. The GID type selects the
address family used on the wire; see
.BR ibv_query_gid_ex (3)
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-chk\fR
validate received buffer
.TP
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-new_send\fR
use the work request builder API rather than
.BR ibv_post_send (3)
.TP
\fB\-Q\fR, \fB\-\-auto\-qpn\fR
let the provider assign the queue pair number rather than supplying
one. Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-pidonfep\fR or \fB\-\-ri\fR
.TP
\fB\-I\fR, \fB\-\-imm\fR=\fIBITS\fR
send with immediate data of \fIBITS\fR bits, either 32 or 64. 64-bit
immediate data requires \fIIBV_DEVICE_IMM64\fR
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-sge\fR=\fIN\fR
scatter/gather the buffer across \fIN\fR entries for send, send with
immediate and receive
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-lkey32\fR
post the scatter/gather list with 32-bit local keys, using
\fIstruct ibv_sge\fR and \fBibv_post_recv\fR(3). By default the list
carries 64-bit local keys in \fIstruct ibv_sge64\fR, flagged with
\fIIBV_SEND_SGE64\fR on send and posted with \fBibv_post_recv64\fR(3),
which requires \fIIBV_DEVICE_KEY64\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-pidonfep\fR=\fIN\fR
use \fIN\fR as the PIDonFEP field of this side's queue pair number
.TP
\fB\-\-ri\fR=\fIN\fR
use \fIN\fR as the resource index field of this side's queue pair
number

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ibv_ru_rma (1),
.BR ibv_rc_pingpong (1),
.BR ibv_wr_post (3),
.BR ibv_post_send (3),
.BR ibv_uet (7)

